- **Breaker table body OCR -- grid-line removal on body cells LANDED in `BreakerTableParser12` / APIv14 (2026-08-18); gaps 2-6 below still open** --
  Gap 1 of the entry below ("Grid lines inside OCR crops") is now fixed. The fix
  lives in **new** modules, leaving the previous stack intact for comparison:
  - `OcrLibrary/BreakerTableParser12.py` (`PARSER_VERSION = "BreakerParser12"`),
    a copy of Parser11 plus the change below. Parser11 is untouched.
  - `OcrLibrary/BreakerTableParserAPIv14.py` (`API_VERSION = "API_14"`), which
    wires `BreakerTableAnalyzer12` + `PanelHeaderParserV11` + **Parser12**.
    APIv13 is retained.
  - `AnvilUplinkCode/uplink_server.py` and `DevEnv/DevTestEnv.py` import APIv14.
  **What the fix does:** module-level `_prep_cell_for_ocr(gray_crop)` removes the
  table's own borders from each body cell immediately before EasyOCR, called from
  `_ocr_body_column_by_rows()` in **both** `SeparatedLayoutParser` and
  `CombinedLayoutParser` (applied to the row band, before the `_HDR_OCR_SCALE`
  resize). It binarizes the cell, opens with a horizontal kernel and a vertical
  kernel, keeps only long/skinny components (full-width horizontal runs anywhere;
  tall thin vertical runs **only within `_CELL_PREP_V_EDGE_ZONE_FR` of the left or
  right edge**, which is what protects a centered `1` from being erased as a
  border), then thin-dilates that mask and `cv2.INPAINT_TELEA`s it -- the same
  approach `BreakerTableAnalyzer12._remove_grids_and_save` uses page-wide.
  A small edge fringe is then flattened to the background level.
  All thresholds are named `_CELL_PREP_*` constants at the top of the file.
  **Coordinate mapping is deliberately preserved:** the crop is never resized or
  offset (the residual fringe is *filled*, not cropped), so the existing
  `_HDR_OCR_SCALE` scale-back and strip→page box math is untouched. Verified by
  the token overlays still landing tightly on the glyphs.
  **Debug support:** with `debug=True`, each body column also writes
  `{base}_parser_body_sep_col{idx}_{role}_cellprep.png` (or
  `..._combined_col{idx}_cellprep.png`) next to the existing `_overlay.png` -- a
  BEFORE | AFTER image of the whole column with every prepped row band written
  back, so grid removal can be eyeballed against the token overlay.
  **Measured result (laptop, 2026-08-18).** `DevEnv/CompareCellPrep_APIv13_vs_APIv14.py`
  runs PageFilter + PanelSearchToolV26 once and then parses the *same* crops with
  APIv13 and APIv14, so differences isolate the parser change. Over 6 PDFs / 28
  panels (`good_tester_combo`, `generic3`, `NoAmps`, `K`, `H`, `chucksmall`,
  `makayla2`): **no panel regressed, no breaker was lost, and review-cell counts
  were identical everywhere.** 24 of 28 panels were bit-identical.
  - **Recovered false negative (the headline):** `H.pdf` panel01, **right side
    row 2**. APIv13 read that trip cell as `'20 A'` at conf **0.39**, just under
    the `_HDR_MIN_CONF` 0.40 accept floor, so the row produced no breaker at all
    (18 breakers, `1P_20A` x15). With the borders removed the same cell reads at
    high confidence, giving 19 breakers / `1P_20A` x16. This panel draws a tight
    box around every cell, so the border was sitting directly against the glyphs
    -- see `.../H/candidate_v14/debug/H_..._panel01_parser_body_sep_col11_trip_cellprep.png`
    and `..._col10_poles_cellprep.png`.
  - **Combined-layout raw text fixed:** `chucksmall` panels 01/02 (38 rows) went
    from EasyOCR emitting `'20 A'` and `'1'` as two tokens with the `/` invisible,
    to a single correct `'20 A / 1'` combo token. Parsed amps/poles are unchanged,
    but the parser no longer has to lean on `_decode_amp_digits`' "lost slash"
    repair (`'201'` → 20) to get there.
  - Also seen: `'20A'` → `'20 A'` normalizations, and on `H` two new `'X'` tokens
    (conf 0.59 / 0.25) exposed once the grid ink stopped fusing with a real X mark.
    Both are harmless -- `_parse_trip_value` / `_parse_poles_value` reject `'X'`,
    the 0.25 one is below the single-char floor, and the breaker tally rose by
    exactly the one recovered row.
  - **Known cost:** long combo tokens carry lower aggregate confidence than the
    short fragments they replace (`'20 A / 1'` at 0.60-0.93 vs `'20 A'` at ~1.00).
    Still comfortably above 0.40 on every observed cell, but this is the thing to
    watch if `_HDR_MIN_CONF` is ever raised (gap 6 below).
  - Artifacts: `DevEnv/CellPrepCompare/<pdf_stem>/comparison.json` plus
    `baseline_v13/` and `candidate_v14/` crop copies with their own `debug/`
    folders (regenerable; PNG/PDF are gitignored).
  **Not addressed here (still open):** gaps 2-6 below, i.e. single OCR pass,
  full-alphabet allowlist on numeric cells, fixed 2x upscale, unwired ALT pole
  voting, and the low single-char confidence floor.

- **Breaker table body OCR reliability -- cell-level EasyOCR with known prep gaps (documented 2026-08-18; gap 1 fixed 2026-08-18, see entry above)** --
  Production breaker-table body values (trip amps, poles) are read by
  `BreakerTableParser11` (`SeparatedLayoutParser` / `CombinedLayoutParser`),
  wired through `BreakerTableParserAPIv13`. **Confirmed behavior:** OCR is
  already **per cell**, not bulk -- each column is cropped once, row bands are
  derived from horizontal grid lines on a reference column per side, and
  `_ocr_body_column_by_rows()` calls EasyOCR once per row band (full column
  width × one row height). Header-band column labels are OCR'd as one strip
  per column (single header row). Misreads may therefore come from **cell prep /
  OCR settings / post-OCR parsing**, not from reading many cells in one call.
  **Diagnosis first:** with `BreakerTablePipeline(debug=True)`, inspect
  `{crop}/debug/{base}_parser_body_sep_col{idx}_{role}_overlay.png` (or
  `_parser_body_combined_col{idx}_overlay.png`) -- each token shows text +
  confidence. Distinguish (a) EasyOCR wrong/missing text, (b) correct OCR
  rejected by `_parse_trip_value` / `_parse_poles_value` / `_parse_combo_cell`
  (e.g. amps must be ≥10 and %5==0), (c) silent wrong value that passes
  validators (e.g. 20→30).
  **Known prep / reliability gaps in production body path:**
  1. **Grid lines inside OCR crops -- FIXED 2026-08-18 in `BreakerTableParser12`
     (see the entry above); the description below is of the Parser11 behavior.**
     Docstrings refer to "gridless" strips,
     but `gray_body = raw_gray` with no grid removal before body OCR
     (`SeparatedLayoutParser.parse`, ~lines 2079-2080). Column x-bounds sit on
     vertical line centers; row bands are inflated to horizontal dividers (~2%
     pad, often still including the line). Edge grid ink can cause digit/border
     confusions (`1`/`|`, `0`/`8`). Elsewhere in the same file,
     `_cell_has_visual_content` / `_detect_visual_blank_placeholder` already
     morphologically remove H/V lines for *review* logic but not for OCR input.
  2. **Single OCR pass per cell** -- body uses one `readtext` call per row
     (`mag_ratio=1.0` after 2× OpenCV upscale). Panel header runs normal +
     inverted passes; `BreakerHeaderFinder` runs mag 1.6 + 2.0; ALT module runs
     up to four passes. No conditional retry on low confidence / empty cell.
  3. **Full alphabet allowlist on numeric cells** -- `_HDR_OCR_ALLOWLIST`
     includes all letters for trip/poles/combo body OCR. Role-specific tighter
     allowlists (ALT uses `"0123456789A "` for numeric columns) are not applied;
     `_normalize_digitish_chars` repairs S/Z→5, O→0, I/L/J/|→1 after the fact.
  4. **Fixed 2× upscale, not target glyph height** -- `_HDR_OCR_SCALE = 2.0`
     regardless of row band height or source DPI; panel-level upscale only when
     H < 1600. Small text on large crops may remain under-resolved for EasyOCR.
  5. **Pole voting ensemble not wired** -- `BreakerTableParserALTModuleized.py`
     has `_vote_poles_digit` (OCR + template + ink/shape features, dash veto);
     `BreakerTableParser11` does not use it.
  6. **Low single-char confidence floor** -- body OCR accepts 1-char tokens at
     `_HDR_MIN_CONF * 0.7` (= 0.28 at default 0.40), which admits weak pole-digit
     reads; raising it trades false positives for false negatives unless paired
     with retry/voting.
  **Suggested fix order (Marco's call):** (1) inset crops and/or
  reuse existing grid-line removal before body OCR -- **DONE 2026-08-18, see the
  entry above**; (2) role-specific allowlists;
  (3) target-height upscale per row band; (4) conditional second pass
  (inverted/higher mag) on empty/low-conf cells; (5) wire ALT pole voting for
  poles columns only. Re-verify on failing panels via debug overlays before/after.
  `DevEnv/CompareCellPrep_APIv13_vs_APIv14.py` is the harness for steps 2-5 as
  well: point it at any two API versions' crops to get a per-panel, per-row diff.

- **STANDING DECISION: the heuristic `PanelSearchToolV25` is ground truth for panel/table detection -- confirmed by Marco 2026-07-06** --
  For all panel-detection / table-detection ML work (TATR v7 and any
  future v8+), the heuristic `PanelSearchToolV25` boxes are treated as
  **ground truth** on the documents currently in hand. This is Marco's
  explicit, standing instruction -- do NOT re-ask it each session.
  Practical implications for the agent:
  - When the ML model disagrees with the heuristic on these PDFs, the
    **ML is wrong** (a false negative / false positive / loose box),
    not the heuristic. "Improving the model" means converging ML
    output toward the heuristic, and mAP is scored with the heuristic
    as GT (as `evaluate_model.py --pdf` already does).
  - Scope caveat recorded deliberately: this holds "at least on the
    documents I have." If a genuinely new document class appears where
    the heuristic is suspected wrong, flag it to Marco rather than
    silently assuming ML is the reference -- but the default, and the
    training/eval target, is heuristic-as-GT.

- **ML Table Detection -- v8 held-out result DONE 2026-07-10 (label-under-coverage fix VALIDATED; v8 beats v7 on true held-out; NOT yet production default)** --
  Resolves the "true held-out generalization is currently unknown" blocker from
  the 2026-07-07 contamination entry below. A clean held-out protocol was
  established and v8 was trained and measured against the locked v7. **Result:
  v8 wins.** The v7 "whole-page blindness" was confirmed to be a **training-label
  under-coverage bug**, not a model-capacity limit, and fixing the labels both
  lifts recall and generalizes to unseen docs.
  - **Root cause (confirmed):** in the v6 dataset (`v6/annotations_v6.json`)
    multi-page docs were barely labeled -- `derek2` had only 1 of 11 pages
    labeled, `derekfirst` only 1 of 10; the remaining ~19 pages were included
    with 0 annotations = **trained as negatives**, so v7 was explicitly taught
    "derek-style page = empty." Fix = **relabel ALL pages from the heuristic
    ground truth** (per the standing `PanelSearchToolV25`-is-GT decision).
  - **v8 training (Paperspace, finished clean 2026-07-10):** v4/v7 stable recipe
    at 28 epochs (`--batch-size 2 --learning-rate 1e-5 --warmup-steps 50
    --weight-decay 0.01 --val-split 0.1 --num-queries 15
    --freeze-backbone-epochs 0`), dataset `v8_holdout/` (428 imgs / 1839 anns:
    28 real pages heuristic-relabeled across ALL pages from 20 docs with
    derek2/derekfirst deliberately held OUT, + 400 dense synthetic).
    `train_runtime` 2691 s, `train_loss` 5.5 -> 0.674, exit 0. Internal
    (10%-mostly-synth) `eval_map_50` rose monotonically 0.266 (ep1, already >
    v7's best-ever 0.2555) -> peak **0.3999 @ epoch 25**, eval_loss 1.37 ->
    ~0.50. No v6-style mode collapse. Production weights = `models_v8/best/`
    (the epoch-25 checkpoint; HF `best_model_checkpoint = checkpoint-4825`).
    **`model.safetensors` sha256 =
    `074a2b0e5cff2e9928c289becf2124aebba15693f55bdb1d76a73d3324711e07`**,
    verified identical on Paperspace and Marco's laptop.
  - **Held-out eval (heuristic = GT, conf 0.5, IoU 0.5), v7 vs v8:**
    - **Set A = `derek2`, `derekfirst`** (v7 TRAINED on these; v8 NEVER saw them
      -- so any v8 win here is despite v7's data advantage): mAP@0.5 avg
      0.295 -> **0.367**; recall 0.281 -> **0.484** (18/64 -> 31/64); FN 46 -> 33.
    - **Set B = 3 `~/Documents/Diagrams/` PDFs** (`chucksmall`,
      `ELECTRICAL SET (Mark Up)`, `Panels_Example`; fully independent, neither
      model trained on): mAP@0.5 avg 0.759 -> **0.817**; recall 0.520 ->
      **0.720** (26/50 -> 36/50); FN 24 -> 14.
    - **All 5 PDFs:** mAP@0.5 avg 0.573 -> **0.637**; recall **0.386 -> 0.588**
      (+52% relative; 44/114 -> 67/114); FN 70 -> 47.
    - **Tradeoff (expected + acceptable):** precision 0.88 -> 0.63 -- v8
      over-predicts and picks up some FPs. Recall (the documented v7 failure
      mode) is what moved most. The one soft regression is `derekfirst`
